 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [LinPrim Optimization](3492-linprim-optimization.md)
-LinPrim Optimization is the parameter update phase of the LinPrim rendering pipeline, which adjusts the geometric and appearance features of linear primitives (triangles) to minimize a differentiable loss function. It differs from standard 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) optimization by backpropagating gradients through a linear primitive rasterizer (MTIA) rather than a Gaussian splatting kernel, requiring specific handling of ray-space to world-space transformations and quaternion-based rotation updates. It builds on [LinPrim Gradient Computation](3038-linprim-gradient-computation.md) to compute the necessary gradients for intersection depths.
+LinPrim Optimization is the training procedure for Linear Primitives, using differentiable rendering to adjust primitive attributes (center, covariance, opacity, color) by backpropagating gradients from a composite L1 and SSIM loss through the entire rendering pipeline. It distinguishes itself from standard Gaussian Splatting by explicitly handling the backpropagation through the Linear Primitive Intersection Algorithm (MTIA) and preprocessing steps, including specific handling for ray-space to world-space transformation and adaptive learning rates based on scene scale. Choose it when training linear primitives for differentiable volumetric rendering.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 **Time and Memory Complexity**
-The method involves computing barycentric coordinates and their derivatives, which are constant time operations per triangle. The transformation to world space is also constant time. The overall complexity is linear with respect to the number of primitives.
